    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Begun by educated Indians seeking reform, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> into a mass movement whose non-violent campaigns drew millions into the struggle against British rule.</li><li>It was never one body: moderates, radicals, revolutionaries, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>, peasants, workers, opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and the people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> pursued different, often conflicting aims.</li><li>British rule ended on 14–15 August 1947 with the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, which uprooted some fifteen million people and was accompanied by killings on a vast scale.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> took no part in the independence celebrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>: he spent 15 August 1947 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, fasting, praying and spinning in a house in a riot-torn district that he shared with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> leader Huseyn Shaheed Suhrawardy.</p></aside>
    </section>

