    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1885</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> holds its first session in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1905</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>’s partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> provokes the Swadeshi movement and the boycott of British goods</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">Troops fire on an unarmed crowd at Jallianwala Bagh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, killing hundreds</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920–1922</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> leads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/non-cooperation-movement">Non-cooperation movement</a>, in alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khilafat-movement">Khilafat movement</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-march">Salt March</a> to Dandi begins a nationwide campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1940</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> demands independent states for the Muslim-majority areas</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1942</span><span class="rf-text">The Congress launches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quit-india-movement">Quit India Movement</a>, and its leaders are imprisoned</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text">British rule ends, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> is partitioned into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji.jpg" alt="Dadabhai Naoroji" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji">Dadabhai Naoroji</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dadabhai_Naoroji_1889.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the 1880s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>, established when the Crown took over the government of India from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>. About three-fifths of the subcontinent formed the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> and a small corps of British officials, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>. The rest belonged to more than five hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, whose rulers kept their thrones by accepting British paramountcy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> was proclaimed Empress of India in 1877.</p><p>British rule also created its own critics. The universities founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, Bombay and Madras in 1857, the railways and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a>, and a growing press in English and the Indian languages produced a new class of lawyers, teachers, journalists and clerks who had learned British liberal ideas and judged the Raj by them. They resented their exclusion from the higher posts of the Indian Civil Service, whose entrance examination was held only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the Ilbert Bill affair of 1883, when protests by Europeans in India forced the government to weaken a measure allowing Indian judges to try Europeans, showed them how far racial privilege reached. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji">Dadabhai Naoroji</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a> from Bombay, argued that British rule drained India’s wealth through the salaries, pensions, interest and profits sent to Britain, at a time when the famine of 1876–1878 killed millions.</p><p>The same decades saw movements of religious and social reform, from the Brahmo Samaj, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy">Ram Mohan Roy</a> in 1828, to the Arya Samaj of Dayananda Saraswati, founded in 1875, which called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>. Sayyid Ahmad Khan, who founded the Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental College at Aligarh in 1875, urged Muslims to embrace Western learning and stay loyal to the British, and he warned them against the Congress, which he feared would speak for the Hindu majority. Regional bodies such as the Indian Association of Calcutta, founded by Surendranath Banerjea in 1876, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a> Sarvajanik Sabha and the Madras Mahajana Sabha prepared the ground for a national organisation. In December 1885 seventy-two delegates met in Bombay for the first session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, encouraged by a retired British official, Allan Octavian Hume, and presided over by the Calcutta barrister Womesh Chunder Bonnerjee.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution.jpg" alt="Lahore Resolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:All_India_Muslim_League_Working_Committee_Lahore_1940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For its first twenty years the Congress sought reform within the empire by petition and argument. Its leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji">Dadabhai Naoroji</a>, Pherozeshah Mehta and Gopal Krishna Gokhale, asked for more Indians in the civil service, elected members in the legislative councils, lower military spending and lighter taxes. A more radical group, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, Bipin Chandra Pal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and Lala Lajpat Rai in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, demanded swaraj, self-rule, and urged swadeshi, the use of Indian goods, together with the boycott of British ones and national education; Tilak’s declaration that swaraj was his birthright became a rallying cry. Revolutionary societies in Bengal and Maharashtra, and the Ghadar Party founded by Punjabi emigrants on the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1913, aimed to overthrow British rule by force.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, who returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1915 after more than twenty years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, set out his ideas in Hind Swaraj (1909). True swaraj, he argued, was self-rule in the moral as well as the political sense, to be won by satyagraha, “truth-force”, the non-violent resistance he had developed against discriminatory laws in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. His constructive programme joined politics to social reform: hand-spinning and the wearing of khadi cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Muslim unity, the removal of untouchability and the uplift of the villages. In December 1929 the Congress, meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, made purna swaraj, complete independence, its goal, and in the 1930s Nehru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> and the Congress Socialists linked independence with land reform and planned industry.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a>, founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> in 1906 to protect the interests of Muslims as a minority, won separate electorates in 1909 and agreed a joint programme with the Congress in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> Pact of 1916. As majority rule came nearer, its leaders sought guarantees and then autonomy for the Muslim-majority provinces. In 1930 the poet Muhammad Iqbal proposed a consolidated Muslim state in north-western India, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> of March 1940 the League under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> demanded “independent states” for the Muslim-majority areas, holding that Hindus and Muslims were two nations. Historians debate how far Jinnah sought a fully sovereign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and how far a bargaining position for parity within a loose Indian union. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> insisted that freedom must end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> oppression; the Hindu Mahasabha and the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh, founded in 1925, promoted Hindu nationalism; and most princes, who met in the Chamber of Princes from 1921, wished to keep their thrones, while popular movements in their states demanded responsible government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/salt-march"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salt-march.jpg" alt="Salt March" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/salt-march">Salt March</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marche_sel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yann, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a>’s partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1905, which created a Muslim-majority eastern province, provoked the Swadeshi movement: mass meetings, the boycott and burning of British cloth, and national schools. Moderates and radicals split in 1907, and young revolutionaries turned to bombs and assassination. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-councils-act-1909">Indian Councils Act 1909</a> gave Muslims separate electorates, and in 1911 the partition was revoked and the capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> more than a million Indians served overseas, and in 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montagu-declaration">Montagu Declaration</a> promised the gradual realisation of responsible government.</p><p>In March 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rowlatt-act">Rowlatt Act</a> extended wartime powers of detention without trial. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> called a nationwide strike against it, and on 13 April, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-dyer">Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer</a> fired without warning on an unarmed crowd, killing 379 people by the official count and many more by Indian estimates. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/non-cooperation-movement">Non-cooperation movement</a> of 1920–1922, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khilafat-movement">Khilafat movement</a> of Indian Muslims in defence of the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, carried the boycott of councils, courts, schools and foreign cloth across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In Malabar in 1921 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mappila">Mappila</a> Muslim rising against British rule and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> landlords, which also killed and forcibly converted Hindus, was crushed by the army with heavy loss of life. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauri-chaura-incident">Chauri Chaura incident</a> of February 1922, in which police fired on a procession and the crowd burned the police station, killing 22 policemen, Gandhi called the campaign off.</p><p>In 1928 the all-British Simon Commission was boycotted; Lala Lajpat Rai died weeks after being beaten in a police baton charge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and Bhagat Singh and his comrades shot dead a young police officer in revenge. On 12 March 1930 Gandhi began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-march">Salt March</a>, walking with 78 followers some 385 kilometres from his ashram at Sabarmati to Dandi, where on 6 April he made salt in defiance of the salt tax. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">Civil disobedience</a> spread across India, and over 60,000 people were jailed; at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> troops fired on unarmed followers of Abdul Ghaffar Khan. A truce, the Gandhi–Irwin Pact of 1931, took Gandhi to the second Round Table Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, but the talks failed, and the renewed campaign was suppressed by 1934.</p><p>The Congress ministries elected in 1937 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1935">Government of India Act 1935</a> resigned in 1939, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> declared India at war without consulting them. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cripps-mission">Cripps Mission</a> failed, the Congress launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quit-india-movement">Quit India Movement</a> on 8 August 1942, and Gandhi called on Indians to “do or die”. The leaders were arrested the next morning; strikes, sabotage, attacks on police stations and local risings followed, and police and troops crushed them, arresting about 100,000 people and killing hundreds.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru.jpg" alt="Jawaharlal Nehru" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nehru_in_the_Netherlands,_1957.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harry Pot, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> (1869–1948), a lawyer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> who had led the resistance of Indians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and then of peasants in Champaran and Kheda, turned the Congress into a mass organisation with village committees and a membership fee of four annas a year, and drew in peasants, women and the poor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> (1889–1964), educated at Harrow and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, became the voice of its socialist and internationalist wing and Gandhi’s chosen heir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a> (1875–1950), who led the peasants of Bardoli in 1928, was its great organiser; Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, an Islamic scholar and journalist, was its president from 1940 to 1946; and the poet Sarojini Naidu became the first Indian woman to preside over it, in 1925. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> (1897–1945), twice Congress president, broke with Gandhi in 1939, escaped from house arrest in 1941 and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-army">Indian National Army</a>, raised with Japanese support largely from Indian prisoners of war, which fought beside the Japanese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a> and Kohima in 1944.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> (1876–1948), a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> barrister once hailed as an ambassador of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Muslim unity, left the Congress in 1920 in opposition to Gandhi’s mass methods and from the mid-1930s rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> into a mass party; his followers called him Quaid-e-Azam, “Great Leader”. Many Muslims opposed partition, among them Azad and the Pashtun leader Abdul Ghaffar Khan, whose Khudai Khidmatgar, “Servants of God”, were sworn to non-violence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> (1891–1956), the foremost leader of the untouchable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, clashed with Gandhi in 1932, when Gandhi fasted against separate electorates for the depressed classes until Ambedkar accepted reserved seats instead in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a> Pact.</p><p>The revolutionaries, among them Bhagat Singh, hanged in 1931 at the age of twenty-three, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandra-shekhar-azad">Chandra Shekhar Azad</a> and Surya Sen, who led the raid on the armouries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> in 1930, became heroes to many young Indians, though Gandhi condemned their violence. Women came forward in great numbers from 1930, making salt, picketing shops and courting arrest. In 1942 Aruna Asaf Ali raised the Congress flag in Bombay after the leaders’ arrest and Usha Mehta ran a secret Congress radio, and Lakshmi Sahgal commanded the Indian National Army’s women’s regiment. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, movements such as Sheikh Abdullah’s National Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> campaigned against their own rulers. The movement’s strength lay in millions of ordinary followers: peasants who refused rents and taxes, mill workers on strike, students who left government colleges and volunteers who went to prison campaign after campaign.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india.jpg" alt="Constitution of India" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constitution_of_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Illumination/ornamentation by Beohar Rammanohar Sinha, calligraphy by Prem Behari Narain Raizada., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in which some 2.5 million Indians served in the army, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> exhausted and in debt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a>’s Labour government, elected in 1945, meant to withdraw. The trials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-army">Indian National Army</a> officers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-fort">Red Fort</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1945–1946 stirred feeling across India, and in February 1946 ratings of the Royal Indian Navy mutinied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>. In the elections of 1945–1946 the Congress won most of the general seats and the League most of those reserved for Muslims. The Cabinet Mission’s plan of May 1946, for a weak centre over groups of provinces, broke down in mutual distrust, and the League’s Direct Action Day on 16 August 1946 began the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> Killing, in which more than 4,000 people died in three days; massacres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> in Noakhali and of Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> followed.</p><p>In February 1947 Attlee announced that Britain would leave by June 1948. The last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Lord Mountbatten</a>, brought the date forward, and on 3 June the leaders of the Congress, the League and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> accepted partition, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>’s wishes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> became independent on 14 August and India on 15 August 1947, when Nehru told the Constituent Assembly at midnight that India would “awake to life and freedom”. The boundaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">Partition of India</a>, drawn by Sir Cyril Radcliffe and published two days later, divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Some fifteen million people crossed the new borders; Hindus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> and Muslims killed one another in massacres and attacks on refugee trains, and tens of thousands of women were abducted. Estimates of the dead range from about 200,000 to two million.</p><p>With British paramountcy at an end, Patel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/v-p-menon">V. P. Menon</a> persuaded almost all the princes whose states lay within India to accede to it before 15 August, while the states within Pakistan’s borders joined Pakistan. India took over Junagadh, whose ruler had chosen Pakistan, and confirmed it by a plebiscite, and annexed Hyderabad after the military action of September 1948 known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-polo">Operation Polo</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> the maharaja, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh">Hari Singh</a>, acceded to India in October 1947 after Pashtun tribesmen from Pakistan invaded, beginning a war and a lasting dispute. Gandhi was shot dead in Delhi on 30 January 1948 by Nathuram Godse, a Hindu nationalist who blamed him for concessions to Muslims and Pakistan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a>, drafted under Ambedkar’s chairmanship, made the country the Republic of India on 26 January 1950, with votes for all adults and untouchability abolished. The Congress had won independence with little armed conflict with Britain, but not the united India it had sought.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr.jpg" alt="Martin Luther King Jr." loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martin_Luther_King,_Jr._and_Lyndon_Johnson_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yoichi Okamoto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s independence began the end of the European empires in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> followed in 1948, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>’s example for his campaign of “positive action” in 1950. Gandhi’s methods of non-violent resistance were studied by the leaders of the civil rights movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>, who visited India in 1959, and by opponents of apartheid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, where Gandhi had first developed them.</p><p>In India the Congress governed without a break until 1977, and the movement’s ideals of parliamentary democracy, equal citizenship regardless of religion or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, and fundamental rights were written into the constitution of 1950. The two successor states remember the struggle in their own ways: India celebrates its independence on 15 August and honours Gandhi as the Father of the Nation, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> celebrates 14 August and honours Jinnah as its founder; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> broke away in 1971 to become Bangladesh. Popular memory in India has also honoured the revolutionaries, above all Bhagat Singh, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a>, whose methods Gandhi had rejected. Partition entered fiction at once, in the stories of Saadat Hasan Manto and Khushwant Singh’s novel Train to Pakistan (1956), though for decades its personal memories were kept mostly within families.</p><p>Historians have long debated the movement’s nature. Nationalist historians such as Bipan Chandra described a broad anti-imperialist struggle of the Indian people. The “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> School” of the 1960s and 1970s, including Anil Seal and John Gallagher, stressed the competition of local elites for power and patronage under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a>. From 1982 the Subaltern Studies historians, led by Ranajit Guha, studied the politics of peasants, workers and tribal peoples, who often acted on their own terms rather than at the bidding of nationalist leaders. Ayesha Jalal’s study of Jinnah questioned whether a separate state had been his aim from the start, and the oral histories of Urvashi Butalia and others brought the experience of refugees and of women into view. Most historians explain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s withdrawal in 1947 by both the movement’s long pressure and the war’s drain on British power.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Indian independence movement, from the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> in 1885 to 1947, joined petitions, mass non-violent campaigns led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and armed revolt against British rule. Divided by religion, class and strategy, it ended British rule but could not hold the subcontinent together, and its methods inspired anti-colonial and civil rights movements across the world.</em></p>

